m=core.governance.reportengine;
i=db:std.db;
i=str:std.str;
i=log:shared.log;
(* shared.types is imported for $lokeerr, which generate's return type still names. *)
i=t:shared.types;
i=s:core.storage.db;

t=$reporttype{
  $compliancesummary:i64;
  $piidetectionlog:i64;
  $routingdecisions:i64;
  $policychanges:i64;
  $costsavings:i64
};

t=$reportfilter{
  startdate:str;
  enddate:str;
  reporttype:str;
  format:str
};

t=$reportrow{
  timestamp:str;
  category:str;
  detail:str;
  severity:str;
  value:str
};

t=$report{
  title:str;
  reporttype:str;
  generatedat:str;
  periodstart:str;
  periodend:str;
  rows:@$reportrow;
  summary:str;
  format:str
};


(* ── what these reports read ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
   The row accessors are core.storage.db's, not std.json's. db.many returns [$row] and
   all five report builders read their fields with json.str against those rows, so every
   governance report came back with empty timestamps and empty details: the row count was
   right and every row was blank (LS1.1i).

   The column names were wrong as well. audit_log has no `timestamp` and no `detail`
   column. Every column named in every query below is now checked against the DDL in
   storage/migrations.tk: created_at, event_type, use_case, model_id, provider,
   sensitivity, risk_tier, cost_usd. There is no rule_id and no user_id in that table,
   which is why nothing here claims to report on either.

   GA5.11 — the window. created_at is epoch milliseconds stored as TEXT; the filter dates
   are ISO. The comparison was string-against-string and matched nothing, on every
   installation, whatever the table held. The bounds are now resolved to epoch
   milliseconds by core.storage.db.msrangeof and bound as numbers, and the column is
   CAST on the way in so SQLite does not convert the parameter back to text. The full
   reasoning for converting the parameter rather than re-representing the column — the
   chain digest covers created_at's stored text — is in msrangeof itself.

   GA5.6 — the event-type filters, as first decided. The five templates filtered on
   `event_type LIKE '%pii%'`, `'%routing%'` and so on, and matched nothing: at the time the
   only event type written was 'llm_request'. GA5.6 dropped the filters, which was right
   then and wrong as soon as the trail grew.

   GA5.30 — the event-type filters, as they stand. The trail now holds, per request on the
   browser route, an `llm_dispatch` row written BEFORE the provider is called (the
   disclosure reservation, DA1.9) and then an outcome row on the same correlation id; plus
   refusals, kill-switch changes and degraded-mode rows. With no filter, one forwarded
   request was two routing decisions and a kill-switch change was a third. The event types
   are therefore named, once each, in the clauses below, and every template says which:

     dispatch outcome    llm_request, llm_request_failed. A provider was called and this
                         is what came of it. One row per attempt: a fallback hop has its
                         own correlation id (`<id>-h<n>`) and its own outcome, because it
                         is its own call to its own provider. The same pair
                         core.governance.scorecard counts (dispatchclause).
     request outcome     the dispatch outcomes, plus every way a request is refused before
                         anything is sent: llm_request_refused, privacy_refused,
                         quota_exceeded, policy_deny, policy_require_approval and
                         kill_switch_refused (a request the switch stopped). Reduced to ONE row per request,
                         the last written, so a request whose first hop failed and whose
                         second answered is one request whose outcome is the answer.
     control change      kill_switch_engaged, kill_switch_released.

   Never counted by any template: llm_dispatch (it precedes the outcome row of the same
   call, so counting both is the double count), degraded_mode_entered and
   degraded_mode_exited (a provider's availability, not a request or a decision),
   kill_switch_change_refused (a change to the switch that was refused, so no control
   changed) and kill_switch_agent_refused (an agent run the switch stopped; agents send
   nothing to a model yet, so it is not a request).

   GA5.32 — kill_switch_refused, then and now. GA5.30 read this event type as two things
   told apart by a colon: a request the switch stopped (hex correlation id) and a refused
   switch change (`<scope>:<target>`). The second never existed: no writer has ever
   audited a refused change. The `<scope>:<target>` rows were core.governance.killswitch's
   recordrefusal, written for a browser request the use-case gate stopped
   (`use_case:browser-chat`, the same id for every such request) and for an agent run the
   executor refused (`agent:<id>`). So the colon dropped real refused requests from the
   compliance summary. Each meaning now has its own event type at the writer
   (killswitch.tk), and kill_switch_refused is a request, keyed on its own id, always.
   Rows written before GA5.32 cannot be renamed (audit_log takes INSERT only, and each
   row's hash covers its event type), so they are read by the convention they were
   written under, and only those rows match it, because no writer produces it now:

     kill_switch_refused, `use_case:%`   a request stopped at the use-case gate. Every
                                         one has the same correlation id, so each row is
                                         its own request (keyed on its rowid).
     kill_switch_refused, `agent:%`      an agent run: excluded, as its new type is.

   The templates:

     compliance_summary  every request outcome in the window, one row per request.
     pii_detection_log   the request outcomes classified CONFIDENTIAL or RESTRICTED, one
                         row per request. That classification is the only record the
                         trail keeps of sensitive content having been seen, and
                         governance/dashboard.tk treats exactly those two values as the
                         privacy-coverage numerator, so the two agree.
     routing_decisions   every dispatch outcome, reported by provider and model.
     policy_changes      every control change. It used to be `event_type LIKE '%policy%'`,
                         which matched policy_deny and policy_require_approval: decisions
                         ABOUT requests, reported as changes TO policy. The kill switch is
                         the only governance control whose changes the trail records;
                         an edit to the policy itself is not recorded, and the summary
                         says so.
     cost_savings        every dispatch outcome, with the cost figure. Cost attaches to a
                         call, and the request path writes cost 0.0 deliberately until
                         LS1.1y, so the total is honestly zero rather than absent.

   DA1.5 in its second location: every row's severity was the literal "INFO" and every
   row's value was the empty string, so a cost report carried no cost. Severity now comes
   from the row's risk_tier and value from the column the template is about. *)


f=knowntemplate(name:str):bool{
  if(str.eq(name;"compliance_summary")){<true}el{};
  if(str.eq(name;"pii_detection_log")){<true}el{};
  if(str.eq(name;"routing_decisions")){<true}el{};
  if(str.eq(name;"policy_changes")){<true}el{};
  if(str.eq(name;"cost_savings")){<true}el{};
  <false
};

f=templatelist():str{
  <str.join(", ";@(
    "compliance_summary";
    "pii_detection_log";
    "routing_decisions";
    "policy_changes";
    "cost_savings"))
};


(* One SQL builder for all five templates. They differed only in an event filter and each
   spelled the SELECT list, the window and the ORDER BY out again — five chances for the
   column drift above to come back in one place and not the others. The window clause and
   the ordering come from core.storage.db so there is exactly one spelling of each. *)
f=auditwindowsql(extrafilter:str):str{
  <str.join("";@(
    "SELECT created_at, event_type, use_case, model_id, provider, sensitivity, risk_tier, ";
    s.auditcostcols();
    " FROM audit_log WHERE ";
    s.createdatwindowclause();
    extrafilter;
    s.createdatdescorder()))
};


(* The two event types that mean a provider was called and answered or failed. *)
f=dispatchoutcometypes():str{
  <"'llm_request','llm_request_failed'"
};

f=dispatchoutcomeclause():str{
  <" AND event_type IN (\(dispatchoutcometypes()))"
};

(* A row that is the outcome of a request, without the leading AND so the dedup subquery
   can use it too. Classified by event type: kill_switch_refused is a request the switch
   stopped, and the other kill-switch refusals have their own types, which are not here.
   The one exception is a row written before GA5.32 (see above): its agent-run refusals
   carry this type under `agent:<id>`, and are left out by that prefix. *)
f=requestoutcomepredicate():str{
  <"(event_type IN (\(dispatchoutcometypes()),'llm_request_refused','privacy_refused','quota_exceeded','policy_deny','policy_require_approval','kill_switch_refused') AND NOT \(legacyagentrefusal()))"
};

f=legacyagentrefusal():str{
  <"(event_type='kill_switch_refused' AND correlation_id LIKE 'agent:%')"
};

(* A fallback hop's correlation id is the request's with `-h<n>` appended; the request id
   itself is hex, so the first "-h" is where the hop suffix starts. A use-case refusal
   written before GA5.32 shares `use_case:browser-chat` with every other, so it is keyed on
   its own rowid: one row, one request. *)
f=requestkeyexpr():str{
  <"CASE WHEN event_type='kill_switch_refused' AND correlation_id LIKE 'use_case:%' THEN 'legacy-row:'||rowid WHEN INSTR(correlation_id,'-h')>0 THEN SUBSTR(correlation_id,1,INSTR(correlation_id,'-h')-1) ELSE correlation_id END"
};

(* One row per request: of each request's outcome rows, the last one written. *)
f=requestoutcomeclause():str{
  let pred=requestoutcomepredicate();
  <" AND \(pred) AND rowid IN (SELECT MAX(rowid) FROM audit_log WHERE \(pred) GROUP BY \(requestkeyexpr()))"
};

f=controlchangeclause():str{
  <" AND event_type IN ('kill_switch_engaged','kill_switch_released')"
};

f=piiclause():str{
  <"\(requestoutcomeclause()) AND UPPER(sensitivity) IN ('CONFIDENTIAL','RESTRICTED')"
};


f=severityof(r:$row):str{
  (* risk_tier is the trail's own severity scale: LOW / MEDIUM / HIGH, written by
     core.governance.types.risklabel. A row with none is reported as UNKNOWN rather than
     promoted to INFO, because "we did not record a risk tier" and "this was routine" are
     different statements. *)
  let tier=s.rowstr(r;"risk_tier");
  if(str.len(tier)==0){
    <"UNKNOWN"
  }el{
    <tier
  }
};

(* cost_usd crosses as HUNDREDTHS in an i64 and is formatted from that integer, with no
   f64 anywhere: str.fromfloat renders 1.50 as "1.5", and shared.types.f64fixed truncates instead of padding, so both
   float routes print $1.5 where they mean $1.50. See core.storage.db.moneyfromhundredths. *)
f=costvalue(r:$row):str{
  <str.concat("$";s.moneyfromhundredths(s.rowauditcostcents(r)))
};

f=rowvalue(r:$row;valuekind:str):str{
  if(str.eq(valuekind;"cost")){<costvalue(r)}el{};
  if(str.eq(valuekind;"sensitivity")){<s.rowstr(r;"sensitivity")}el{};
  if(str.eq(valuekind;"provider")){
    <str.join(" / ";@(s.rowstr(r;"provider");s.rowstr(r;"model_id")))
  }el{};
  <severityof(r)
};

f=rowdetail(r:$row):str{
  <str.join(" / ";@(
    s.rowstr(r;"event_type");
    s.rowstr(r;"use_case");
    s.rowstr(r;"model_id")))
};

f=buildrows(results:@$row;category:str;valuekind:str):@$reportrow{
  let rows=mut.@();
  lp(let i=0;i<results.len;i=i+1){
    let r:$row=results.get(i);
    rows=rows.push($reportrow{
      timestamp:s.rowstr(r;"created_at");
      category:category;
      detail:rowdetail(r);
      severity:severityof(r);
      value:rowvalue(r;valuekind)
    })
  };
  <rows
};

(* Summed in integer hundredths, not f64: an integer of cents is the only form in which a
   sum of money is exact. *)
f=costtotalhundredths(results:@$row):i64{
  let total=mut.0;
  lp(let i=0;i<results.len;i=i+1){
    let r:$row=results.get(i);
    total=total+s.rowauditcostcents(r)
  };
  <total
};

f=summaryof(results:@$row;label:str;valuekind:str):str{
  let n=str.fromint(results.len);
  if(str.eq(valuekind;"cost")){
    let totalstr=s.moneyfromhundredths(costtotalhundredths(results));
    <"\(label): \(n), total cost $\(totalstr)"
  }el{
    <"\(label): \(n)"
  }
};

f=buildreport(results:@$row;filter:$reportfilter;reporttype:str;title:str;category:str;valuekind:str;label:str;note:str):$report{
  let summary=summaryof(results;label;valuekind);
  <$report{
    title:title;
    reporttype:reporttype;
    (* Was the literal "now". str.nowiso8601 is what governance/dashboard.tk stamps its
       scorecard with, so the two are comparable. *)
    generatedat:str.nowiso8601();
    periodstart:filter.startdate;
    periodend:filter.enddate;
    rows:buildrows(results;category;valuekind);
    summary:str.concat(summary;note);
    format:filter.format
  }
};

(* A report that ran and found nothing, versus a report whose window could not be read,
   are different facts and a reader has to be able to tell them apart. msrangeof refused
   the dates; this says so in the summary instead of returning an empty report that looks
   like a quiet period.

   It is not an $err. T-18: the error arm of a user-declared sum is dead, so a caller
   matching `$report!$lokeerr` takes the $ok arm whatever this function returns. An $err
   here would reach cli/report.tk as a success carrying an unbound $report — garbage at
   best. The same reasoning applies to unknowntemplatereport below. *)
f=badwindowreport(filter:$reportfilter):$report{
  <$report{
    title:"Report not generated";
    reporttype:filter.reporttype;
    generatedat:str.nowiso8601();
    periodstart:filter.startdate;
    periodend:filter.enddate;
    rows:@();
    summary:"The reporting window could not be read as dates, so no period was searched. Expected ISO dates such as 2026-01-01. Given: \(filter.startdate) to \(filter.enddate)";
    format:filter.format
  }
};

f=unknowntemplatereport(filter:$reportfilter):$report{
  <$report{
    title:"Report not generated";
    reporttype:filter.reporttype;
    generatedat:str.nowiso8601();
    periodstart:filter.startdate;
    periodend:filter.enddate;
    rows:@();
    summary:"Unknown report template: \(filter.reporttype). Available templates: \(templatelist())";
    format:filter.format
  }
};

f=queryfailedreport(filter:$reportfilter;title:str;what:str):$report{
  <$report{
    title:title;
    reporttype:filter.reporttype;
    generatedat:str.nowiso8601();
    periodstart:filter.startdate;
    periodend:filter.enddate;
    rows:@();
    summary:"The audit log could not be queried for \(what). No rows were read, which is not the same as no events having occurred.";
    format:filter.format
  }
};


(* The one query path. Every template goes through here, so the window is resolved once,
   in one place, and a refused window cannot be mistaken for an empty result. *)
f=runreport(filter:$reportfilter;extrafilter:str;reporttype:str;title:str;category:str;valuekind:str;label:str;note:str):$report{
  let range=s.msrangeof(filter.startdate;filter.enddate);
  if(range.ok){}el{
    <badwindowreport(filter)
  };
  let sql=auditwindowsql(extrafilter);
  let params=@(str.fromi64(range.startms);str.fromi64(range.endms));
  <mt db.many(sql;params) {
    $ok:results buildreport(results;filter;reporttype;title;category;valuekind;label;note);
    $err:e queryfailedreport(filter;title;label)
  }
};


f=generatecompliance(conn:i64;filter:$reportfilter):$report{
  <runreport(
    filter;
    requestoutcomeclause();
    "compliance_summary";
    "Compliance Summary Report";
    "compliance";
    "sensitivity";
    "Requests audited, one row per request";
    "")
};

f=generatepii(conn:i64;filter:$reportfilter):$report{
  <runreport(
    filter;
    piiclause();
    "pii_detection_log";
    "PII Detection Log";
    "pii";
    "sensitivity";
    "Requests handling confidential or restricted content";
    "")
};

f=generaterouting(conn:i64;filter:$reportfilter):$report{
  <runreport(
    filter;
    dispatchoutcomeclause();
    "routing_decisions";
    "Routing Decisions Report";
    "routing";
    "provider";
    "Provider calls, one row per call";
    "")
};

f=generatepolicychanges(conn:i64;filter:$reportfilter):$report{
  <runreport(
    filter;
    controlchangeclause();
    "policy_changes";
    "Policy Changes Report";
    "policy";
    "risk";
    "Kill-switch changes recorded";
    ". The kill switch is the only control whose changes the audit trail records. An edit to the policy itself is NOT RECORDED, so its absence here does not mean none occurred")
};

f=generatecost(conn:i64;filter:$reportfilter):$report{
  <runreport(
    filter;
    dispatchoutcomeclause();
    "cost_savings";
    "Cost and Savings Report";
    "cost";
    "cost";
    "Provider calls";
    ". Cost is recorded as 0.0 on every request until LS1.1y wires the provider cost estimate, so a total of $0.00 is what the trail holds rather than what was spent")
};


(* The dispatch was `let result=mut.generatecost(conn;filter)` followed by a chain of ifs
   with an empty final else, so cost_savings worked only by being the initialiser and
   `--template nonsense` quietly produced a cost report under whatever name was asked
   for. Every template is now dispatched explicitly and an unrecognised one is named.

   The return type stays `$report!$lokeerr` because cli/report.tk matches it, but the
   $err arm is NOT used and cannot be: T-18 means a caller matching a user-declared
   error union always takes the first arm, so an $err would be read as an $ok carrying
   an unbound value. Everything this function needs to report is therefore carried
   inside a real $report. knowntemplate() is exported as the plain-bool predicate a
   caller should use if it wants to reject a template before generating anything. *)
f=generate(conn:i64;filter:$reportfilter):$report!$lokeerr{
  log.info(str.concat("generating report: ";filter.reporttype);"report_engine";"system");
  if(knowntemplate(filter.reporttype)){}el{
    log.warn(str.concat("unknown report template: ";filter.reporttype);"report_engine";"system");
    <$ok(unknowntemplatereport(filter))
  };
  if(str.eq(filter.reporttype;"compliance_summary")){
    <$ok(generatecompliance(conn;filter))
  }el{};
  if(str.eq(filter.reporttype;"pii_detection_log")){
    <$ok(generatepii(conn;filter))
  }el{};
  if(str.eq(filter.reporttype;"routing_decisions")){
    <$ok(generaterouting(conn;filter))
  }el{};
  if(str.eq(filter.reporttype;"policy_changes")){
    <$ok(generatepolicychanges(conn;filter))
  }el{};
  if(str.eq(filter.reporttype;"cost_savings")){
    <$ok(generatecost(conn;filter))
  }el{};
  (* GA5.14. Every name knowntemplate admits has its own branch above, so this line is
     unreachable today. It says unknown rather than cost on purpose: the previous spelling
     ended in a bare generatecost call, which meant the day somebody added a sixth name to
     knowntemplate without adding a branch here, that template would quietly render a cost
     report under its own title. An unreachable honest answer costs one line; a silent
     wrong report costs a compliance submission. *)
  log.warn(str.concat("report template has no generator: ";filter.reporttype);"report_engine";"system");
  <$ok(unknowntemplatereport(filter))
};
